<!--
  OZX APM: keep the whole SDK under managed code stripping (APM-7).

  The SDK reaches some of its code only indirectly (event types serialized
  by type tests, the UnityEngine log callback, AndroidJavaObject calls into
  the Android framework), and a stripped SDK fails silently in a release
  build. Unity does not read link.xml files inside packages on every
  version, so OzxApm.Editor.LinkXmlProvider also hands this content to the
  linker (IUnityLinkerProcessor); a copy in the game's Assets folder is not
  needed. Cost: the SDK's own code is kept whole (about 100 KB of IL).

  UnityEngine.TextAsset: ApmClient adds its component at run time
  (AddComponent<ApmClient> on a new GameObject), which needs the engine's
  MonoScript class (class ID 115). With "Strip Engine Code", the linker keeps
  that native class only while the managed TextAsset type (MonoScript's base
  in the player) survives; a game whose scenes hold no script component and
  no TextAsset lost it (APM-7 test app: "Could not produce class with ID
  115" 118 times at startup). Keeping the type keeps MonoScript registered.
-->
<linker>
  <assembly fullname="OzxApm" preserve="all"/>
  <assembly fullname="UnityEngine.CoreModule">
    <type fullname="UnityEngine.TextAsset" preserve="all"/>
  </assembly>
</linker>
